Split GPU thread: plain 3D register writes and constant-buffer data writes travel from the front
in blocks. (ProsperoEden eglantine-a42b4d4-ps5.d 0082 split-register-blocks and 0085
split-cb-data-blocks.)

A plain register write (not in the execution mask) goes into one Regs ring entry of up to 64
(method, argument) words, written straight into reserved ring space and committed before any
other entry, a publish or a drain. The back applies a block through the engine's method sink
(ConsumeSinkImpl: shadow RAM, same-value skip, dirty flags) at once, so nothing sent after it sees
the registers without it. CB_DATA writes go the same way in CbData entries: the back runs each as
CallMethod would (shadow RAM, register, dirty flags) and writes the run to guest memory once
(Maxwell3D::ProcessCBDataBlock). ProsperoEden's census showed 28.7 plain writes and 11.3 CB data
entries a draw, each a ring entry and a CallMethod on the back. EDEN_NX2_REG_BLOCKS=0 sends them
one by one as before. (ProsperoEden keeps CB data writes unbatched with its opt-in payload-release
forwarding, which EglantinePS5 does not have.)

